@node Compilation Pipeline
@chapter Understanding the Compilation Pipeline

@section Standard Pipeline Overview

The EIGHTBOL compilation process follows this pipeline:

@enumerate
@item @strong{Lexer} --- Tokenization and comment handling
@item @strong{Parser} --- Grammar parsing (CL-YACC for most languages)
@item @strong{AST Transformation} --- Canonical plist-based AST creation
@item @strong{Transpile} --- Optional intermediate language transformation
@item @strong{Serialize} --- Write AST to .eightbol file
@item @strong{Backend} --- Code generation for target processor
@item @strong{Assembly} --- Final assembly code output
@end enumerate

@section Key Pipeline Components

@subsection Lexer (frontend-*-lexer.lisp)
Performs tokenization, keyword recognition, operator classification, and
inline COPY expansion at lexical analysis time.

@subsection Parser (frontend-*-parser.lisp)
Implements grammar parsing using CL-YACC (15/17 languages) or custom parsers
(Forth, Goal, Fountain). Produces parse trees that are immediately transformed
to canonical AST format.

@subsection AST Transformation
Parser actions invoke @code{make-*-node} functions that create canonical
plist-based AST nodes. This ensures all backends can process output from any
frontend.

@subsection Backend (backend-*.lisp)
Reads serialized AST from .eightbol file and generates processor-specific
assembly code. Backends are self-contained and can be developed independently.

@node Frontend Architecture
@chapter Frontend Architecture and Implementation

@section Architecture Overview

All EIGHTBOL frontends follow a consistent architecture:

@enumerate
@item Source input (text, from file or stream)
@item Lexical analysis with COPY expansion
@item Syntax parsing to intermediate parse tree
@item Semantic analysis and AST transformation
@item Optional transpilation step
@item Canonical AST plist creation
@item Serialization to .eightbol file
@end enumerate

@section Frontend Phases

@subsection Phase 1: Lexical Analysis
Tokenizes input, expands COPY statements, tracks source location for error
reporting. All frontends perform this phase.

@subsection Phase 2: Parsing
Builds parse tree according to language grammar. Most languages use CL-YACC
(15/17); Forth uses recursive descent; Goal uses custom semantic parser;
Fountain uses domain-specific parser.

@subsection Phase 3: AST Transformation
Converts parse tree to canonical AST format using @code{make-*-node} functions.
This produces language-independent plist structure that all backends understand.

@subsection Phase 4: Optional Transpilation
6 frontends (BASIC, Objective-C, and others) include transpilation phase to
convert to intermediate representation or intermediate language before passing
to backends.

@subsection Phase 5: AST Serialization
Write canonical AST to .eightbol file in @file{Object/Classes/} directory for
later processing by backends.

@section Implementation Patterns

All frontends follow these patterns:

@itemize @bullet
@item Error class: @code{source-error} with source location tracking
@item Parser actions create canonical AST nodes via @code{make-*-node}
@item Reference modification syntax: @code{name(start:length)}
@item Subscript syntax: @code{array(index)}
@item No explicit return values in procedure calls
@end itemize

