@node Development Guidelines
@chapter Development Guidelines and Best Practices

@section Code Organization

@subsection Lisp Style
@itemize @bullet
@item Use hyphenated-names for symbols
@item Use @code{+constants+} with crosses
@item Use @code{*specials/dynamics*} with earmuffs
@item Use @code{percent-for-private%} functions
@item Use @code{_underscore-for-ignored} parameters
@end itemize

@subsection Assembly Style
PascalCase naming conventions for all labels and symbols.

@subsection COBOL/EIGHTBOL Style
Header-Case-Capitalized names for routines, paragraphs, and variables.

@subsection C/Java Style
PascalCase types, snake_case functions (C), camelCase methods/properties (Java).

@section Comment Guidelines

@itemize @bullet
@item Line comments: Use @code{;;} with normal indentation
@item Section headers: Use @code{;;;} or @code{;;;;} for major sections
@item End-of-line comments: Column 32--72 range
@item Assembly end-of-line: Column 32--72, promote long comments to block form
@item COBOL comments: All comments begin with @code{*} in column 7
@end itemize

@section Formatting Guidelines

@itemize @bullet
@item Prefer newline/fresh-line at @strong{start} of line: @code{"~%content"}
@item Use @code{~10t} for indentation instead of literal spaces
@item Follow existing project style
@item Maintain backward compatibility
@end itemize

@section Function Design

@itemize @bullet
@item Lisp functions: 1--2 positional arguments (rarely 3), then keywords
@item Follow SOLID principles
@item Don't repeat yourself (DRY)
@item Maintain existing interface compatibility
@item Document non-obvious logic with docstrings
@end itemize

@node Troubleshooting
@chapter Troubleshooting and Common Issues

@section Parser Errors

@subsection Unexpected Token
Ensure correct language syntax. Check lexer tokenization with @code{trace}
on lexer functions.

@subsection Reference Modification Syntax
Use @code{name(start:length)} for reference modification. Note: start is
1-indexed, length is number of characters.

@subsection Subscripted Access
Use @code{array(index)} syntax. Array indexing is 1-based in COBOL.

@section Backend Issues

@subsection Unsupported Features
DIVIDE and MULTIPLY are not supported. Use bit shift operations instead
or target 65c816 or higher.

@subsection CPU Constraints
Some CPUs have limitations. See CPU-specific sections in main documentation.

@subsection Address Calculation
For complex addressing modes, verify with assembly output inspection.

@section Compilation Failures

@subsection Copybook Not Found
Ensure COPY statement references valid copybook file in include path.
Use @code{-I} flag to add library paths.

@subsection AST Serialization
Verify .eightbol file can be read. Check for corrupted serialization.

@section Testing

@subsection Test Failures
Run individual test to isolate issue:
@example
(fiveam:run! :specific-test-name)
@end example

Review test output for detailed error messages and stack traces.

@section Performance

@subsection Slow Compilation
Check for infinite loops in transpilation or backend phases.
Enable tracing on specific functions to identify bottlenecks.

@section Integration

@subsection Multi-file Compilation
Ensure copybook search paths are set correctly. Test with @code{-I} flags.

